course: dictionary
lesson: 11615
title: A formal word: apprise
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound like they belong in a letter from a head teacher. Apprise is one of them. When you meet a new word, read the words around it, because they are its context and they leave clues. «Please keep me apprised of any changes» is a request, so you can guess it has something to do with telling or news.
explain: Look at the clues in «The guard apprised the king of the enemy's arrival.» The guard has news about an enemy coming. The king needs to hear it. So apprise means to tell someone about something, or inform them.
explain: Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it. «Please keep me informed of any changes» still makes sense, so apprise and inform mean nearly the same. Apprise is the more formal word, so writers use it in letters, reports and stories where people speak stiffly and politely.
fact: Apprise means to tell someone about something, or to inform them.
fact: «Apprise» is formal, so you will mostly see it in letters, reports and official notices.
fact: «The head apprised the parents of the trip» means the head told the parents about the trip.
fact: Apprise is a verb, so it changes: apprise, apprises, apprised, apprising.
fact: Do not mix up apprise with appraise, which means judge how much something is worth.
exercise: «Please keep me apprised of any changes.» What does apprised mean here? | Thrown away | *Told about | Hidden | Lost
exercise: «The guard apprised the king of the enemy's arrival.» Who was told the news? | The enemy | *The king | The castle wall | The guard's horse
exercise: Which word means nearly the same as apprise? | Forget | Hide | *Inform | Dance
test: «The nurse apprised the family of the good news.» What did the nurse do? | Sang a song to the family | *Told the family the news | Wrote a poem about the weather | Lost the news
test: Which sentence uses apprise to mean tell someone news? | The river apprised the bridge of the rain. | *The head will apprise us of the sports day. | I apprised a big red apple at lunch. | The cat apprised the sofa of its nap.
exam: «They were apprised of the rules before the match.» What does apprised mean? | Forgotten | Played | *Told about | Painted
